# What a row stores into the value of a promise is settled first. An object that nobody has seen may be a promise, or answer to `then`: the promise then holds what that one gives, which is anything.
#   function Kept() { return sink(); }
#   function later() { return new Kept(); }
#   function show(v) { return v; }
#   function showFirst(v) { return v; }
#   async function use() { show(await Promise.resolve(1).then(later)); }
#   async function useAll() { showFirst((await Promise.all([new Kept()]))[0]); }
#   use();  useAll();
# What a plain function returns to `new` counts only if it is an object: of `anything` the objects that nobody has seen are left, without the primitives. Nothing of this program goes anywhere, so its promises stay as they were made.
